// How a module's 'use' of an enum is written.
//
// Parse stage only. The statement folds the enum's variants in as names of the
// module, and what may follow the enum -- a list with 'as', a block, 'but' -- is
// a type body's 'use' exactly, so it is covered there. What is particular to the
// module's statement is where its 'pub' goes and what naming the enum already
// asks for. enum-nameres-use holds what the fold refuses once the enum is known.

enum Colors {
  Red, Green, Blue;
}

// The bindings are the fold's, so their visibility is the fold's to say, and every
// fold clause says it the one way: 'use pub'. A 'pub' before the statement would
// be a second spelling of that.
pub use Colors;           //~ ErrorBadPub:5 "write 'use pub'"

// Naming the enum is what asks for its variants, so every one of them is the
// default and '*' adds nothing
use Colors *;             //~ ErrorBadFold:12 "says nothing more"
